AB  - This article explores the concept of the "open source city" as a model for transnational municipalism and democratic regeneration. It examines experiences in cities like Barcelona, Madrid, and Guadalajara, where citizen participation and free online tools are being used to challenge neoliberalism and build a "geopolitics of the commons." The article highlights the potential of cities to lead on issues such as refugee support and environmental protection, even exceeding traditional municipal powers. It contrasts the open source city with the "smart city" model, which relies on proprietary technology and mass surveillance, advocating for open data, democratic participation, and citizen-driven initiatives.
