AB  - This Amnesty International report investigates the "Sensing project" in Roermond, Netherlands, a predictive policing initiative using data and algorithms to anticipate crime. The report finds that the project, designed to prevent 'mobile banditry' (property crime), involves mass surveillance, collects data indiscriminately, and disproportionately targets people of Eastern European origin, particularly Roma individuals, leading to discrimination and privacy violations. The report criticizes the project's flawed design, lack of transparency, and the misuse of the Road Traffic Act for discriminatory stops and searches. It argues that predictive policing projects like Sensing, developed without adequate legal frameworks, pose significant risks to human rights.
