AB  - This letter from undersigned civil society organizations to the Czech Presidency of the Council of the European Union outlines critical shortcomings in the Council's position on the Artificial Intelligence (AI) Act. The organizations highlight missing fundamental rights protections, including inadequate rights and redress mechanisms for individuals affected by AI, insufficient transparency and accountability obligations for high-risk AI, and a need for balanced civil society participation. They also call for comprehensive prohibitions on AI systems posing unacceptable risks, such as remote biometric identification and predictive policing. Key concerns with the Council's current text include a narrowed definition of AI, loopholes in transparency for law enforcement and migration, broad exemptions for national security, and diluted expertise requirements for enforcement authorities. The letter urges the Council to address these issues to ensure robust fundamental rights protection in the final AI Act.
